=== Psalm 16
--- Psalm 16 [title]
overline: The Book of Psalms
heading: Psalm 16
sub: The preservation of the saints • The risen Christ
refs: Psalm 16:1

--- Preserve me, O God
kicker: Psalm 16 · Preserve me
big: Psalm 16:1 | mark: Preserve me, O God
p: **Psalm 16** presents another sacred hymn celebrating the Lord's salvation, the eternal security of the believer, and the promise of the risen Christ.
p: The preservation of the saints is the hope of all who place their trust in God. Our very being cries out to Him for salvation, recognizing He is Lord.

--- Thou art my Lord
kicker: Psalm 16 · Thou art my Lord
big: Psalm 16:2 | mark: Thou art my Lord
p: Note here that this isn't "Lordship salvation," a false gospel that says you must "make Jesus the Lord of your life" to be saved. It is saved believers, already trusting Him to save us, recognizing that He IS the Lord, as a statement of fact.

--- To the saints that are in the earth
kicker: Psalm 16 · The excellent
big: Psalm 16:3 | mark: to the saints that are in the earth
p: David writes, *Thou art my Lord: my goodness extendeth not to thee; But to the saints that are in the earth, and to the excellent, in whom is all my delight.* The good that we do isn't for Him, it adds nothing to the holy and perfect God.
p: The idea that Jesus needs us to make Him Lord, that by our "turning from sin," we justify ourselves before God, is hubris. It's a false gospel.

--- Let your light so shine
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 16:3
p: The good we do comes from Him, and is poured out to the brothers and sisters, the saints. We benefit others, and they glorify God. We obey His judgements, to one another's profit.
big: Matthew 5:16 | mark: glorify your Father which is in heaven
p: We submit ourselves to the righteousness of God. We do not seek to establish our own righteousness, but to magnify His.
scripture: Romans 10:3-4 | mark: going about to establish their own righteousness

--- That ye love one another
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 16:3
p: David's burden is for the saints in the earth, his delight is to the excellent of God. Just as we are commanded to.
scripture: John 13:34-35 | mark: That ye love one another
p: While we should seek to live peaceably with all men, we should especially look with love upon our fellow believers.
scripture: Galatians 6:9-10 | mark: especially unto them who are of the household of faith

--- The message from the beginning
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 16:3
p: This is the message that we have heard from the beginning. It is our job as Christians. It is how we are to know one another. Not keeping to ourselves or seeking our own profit. Certainly not by hating our brothers.
scripture: 1 John 3:10-11 | mark: that we should love one another

--- To delight in others
kicker: Application • Humility
p: This is the mind of Christ, to be servants, one to another. To delight in others.
p: The ultimate expression of humility isn't thinking less of yourself, but not thinking of yourself at all. And the humbleness of submission to God's righteousness should naturally turn to our service for others. Not to add to God's goodness our filthy rags, but to glorify His goodness in us.

--- Drink offerings of blood
kicker: Psalm 16 · Another god
big: Psalm 16:4 | mark: their drink offerings of blood will I not offer
p: Continuing in **Psalm 16**, David calls out the vanity and ultimate end of those that call not upon the Lord for salvation. Who go after another god.
p: They make drink offerings of blood, and speak other names in their prayers.
p: The most prominent example of this today is Roman Catholicism. They make drink offerings, claiming it to be the blood of Christ, profaning His name, and bow to and serve idols representing their own gods.

--- The portion of my cup
kicker: Psalm 16 · My inheritance
big: Psalm 16:5 | mark: The LORD is the portion of mine inheritance and of my cup
p: But for the saved, the Lord is our inheritance, we are co-heirs with Christ the Bible says, and He is our cup, the blood shed for us on the cross:
big: Luke 22:20 | mark: This cup is the new testament in my blood

--- Once for all
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 16:5
p: The very new testament which we preach is founded upon the blood shed by Jesus. Not a chalice of gold held up by a priest to an image, sacrificing Jesus daily, as if that were possible or necessary, to call Him down from heaven again.
p: And it is God that maintains our lot. Who holds us by His power. Not carnal sacrifices made over and over again.
scripture: Hebrews 10:10-14 | mark: once for all; by one offering he hath perfected for ever them that are sanctified

--- A goodly heritage
kicker: Psalm 16 · I shall not be moved
scripture: Psalm 16:6-9 | mark: I have a goodly heritage; because he is at my right hand, I shall not be moved; my flesh also shall rest in hope
p: This is our goodly heritage. This is the promise of God. The Son at the right hand of the Father. We shall not be moved. And so we rejoice.
p: We rest in hope.

--- Thou wilt not leave my soul in hell
kicker: Psalm 16 · The Holy One
big: Psalm 16:10
p: The Psalm is quoted in the preaching of Peter before the crowds at Pentecost.

--- David speaketh concerning him
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 16:10
scripture: Acts 2:25-28 | mark: For David speaketh concerning him

--- The path of life
kicker: Psalm 16 · Fulness of joy
big: Psalm 16:11 | mark: Thou wilt shew me the path of life
p: The New Testament gives context to the Old, if it wasn't obvious already, the meaning in the psalm. We know David was speaking of Jesus. David, looking forward to the Christ, his savior and Lord. Seeing Jesus in all things, that blessed hope we rest in.
p: And because Jesus was not left in hell and His Holy One did not see corruption, we have the promise of eternal life, by His resurrection. So neither will we be left to Hell. We can rejoice knowing that He will forever be before us. Amen

=== Psalm 17
--- Psalm 17 [title]
overline: The Book of Psalms
heading: Psalm 17
sub: Importunity • The apple of the eye • Satisfied with thy likeness
refs: Psalm 17:1

--- Hear the right, O Lord
kicker: Psalm 17 · Importunity
big: Psalm 17:1 | mark: attend unto my cry
p: **Psalm 17** again shows us the model of importunity in prayer.
p: Importunity is a noun that means asking for something over and over again in a strong, pushy, or annoying way. It is the act of persistent begging or making urgent demands that wear people down. David utilizes this in Psalms to great effect. Pursuing an answer from the throne of God, with repeated supplication.
p: It may seem contrary to the fear of the Lord and humility before Him, but scripture shows it is effective. Jesus taught us this as well.

--- Men ought always to pray
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 17:1
scripture: Luke 18:1-8 | mark: men ought always to pray, and not to faint

--- Pray without fainting
kicker: Application • Importunity
p: We often find ourselves in difficult situations, where we have not because we ask not. We also tend to move on, rather than "bother God" with whatever it is we need of Him.
p: Praying without ceasing, staying strong in prayer because we are weak, this is pleasing to the Lord. So we should pray without fainting, and in doing so, exercise our faith that should Christ return in our time, He finds that faith upon the earth.

--- Imprecatory prayers are Biblical
kicker: Application • Imprecatory prayer
p: Notice too, that the woman before the unjust judge is pleading for vengeance against her adversary, just as David is here in this psalm. Just as the martyred under the throne in Heaven plead with God for His wrath to pour out.
p: Imprecatory prayers are Biblical. Acknowledging God's ownership of vengeance, while suffering for His sake, is an act of great faith. And as we have seen previously in **Psalms,** patience is a virtue that leads us to godliness.
p: Importunity in prayer, and imprecatory prayer feels wrong to many. I have seen supposed Christian teachers make the claim that there is no room for this in the New Testament. But they are a mighty tool in our arsenal of faith.

--- Let my sentence come forth
kicker: Psalm 17 · Judgment
big: Psalm 17:2 | mark: Let my sentence come forth from thy presence
p: The psalmist calls for the Lord's judgment, that His sentence be given against the wicked. But it is foolish to cry out for God's perfect justice to be administered, while you yourself are guilty.
p: It is like a thief going to the police because his partners robbed him of his share of their stolen things. Of a drug dealer complaining about being ripped off in a deal gone wrong.
p: And so, David wisely shows us the pattern of praying imprecatory prayers includes self-examination and willingness for God to judge him the same way he is judging others.

--- The paths of the destroyer
kicker: Psalm 17 · Thou hast proved mine heart
scripture: Psalm 17:3-4 | mark: Thou hast proved mine heart; by the word of thy lips I have kept me from the paths of the destroyer
p: David credits God's word to keep him from the *paths of the destroyer.* A key doctrine, that it is God's word that we judge ourselves and others by, not our own feelings or desires. We judge righteously when we allow God to tell us good from evil.

--- Hold up my goings
kicker: Psalm 17 · In thy paths
big: Psalm 17:5 | mark: Hold up my goings in thy paths
p: David then calls for the Lord to hold up his goings, to keep him on the right path.
p: So we see that it isn't simply cursing your enemies and expecting God to overlook your sin. But praying for God's judgement, knowing He is just and seeking His confirmation that you are blameless in the matter. Also, praising Him for keeping you from sin in the first place.
p: All glory goes to God.

--- At the heart of your prayer life
kicker: Application • Humility in prayer
p: Having the faith to be judged fairly by God, knowing that because of God you have walked in His ways, but accepting any fault of your own that He may find...this humility should be at the heart of your prayer life.

--- Thou wilt hear me
kicker: Psalm 17 · Thy right hand
scripture: Psalm 17:6-7 | mark: for thou wilt hear me, O God; O thou that savest by thy right hand
p: Continuing in **Psalms 17**, we see that David's importunity comes from his faith in God to hear those who call upon Him. That we may go boldly before the throne of grace, because He has shown us that He listens to our cries. The initial call for salvation in the Lord's name gives us the hope that He listens to us for far less important matters, such as our suffering in this life.
p: The Lord's lovingkindness is already apparent to those that put their trust in Him, that have been saved by His strong right-hand. Christ Jesus. We know that our Savior is at the right hand of the Father. The New Testament is clear on this. But David is writing hundreds of years earlier, showing that Old Testament saints knew and understood the concept, even if they didn't have the full revelation and details.

--- The apple of the eye
kicker: Psalm 17 · Under the shadow of thy wings
scripture: Psalm 17:8-9 | mark: Keep me as the apple of the eye, hide me under the shadow of thy wings
p: Keep me, O Lord. It is His power that keeps the children of God under His protection and secure in His promises. We shall not fear, because we are under His mighty wing, as a mother hen keeps her chicks.
p: Jesus laments the unfaithful Jews for refusing to be gathered thus.

--- As a hen gathereth her chickens
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 17:8
scripture: Matthew 23:37-39 | mark: even as a hen gathereth her chickens under her wings, and ye would not

--- They are His sword
kicker: Psalm 17 · The wicked
scripture: Psalm 17:10-13 | mark: deliver my soul from the wicked, which is thy sword
p: And His protection is needed in this world, where they hate us for His holy name. Where we are often at the mercy of forces beyond our reckoning. Where the judgment of God against our nation is executed by the hands of the wicked. They are His sword.

--- Their portion in this life
kicker: Psalm 17 · Men of the world
big: Psalm 17:14 | mark: which have their portion in this life
p: They may profit in this life. They may have riches, while we are poor. They may have children and an inheritance. But we have the eternal promise of Christ, which is far more precious than any treasure of this life.

--- When I awake, with thy likeness
kicker: Psalm 17 · Satisfied
big: Psalm 17:15 | mark: I shall be satisfied, when I awake, with thy likeness
p: This is the eternal perspective that Christians should seek. That our satisfaction is in the resurrection, and the current, temporal troubles are nothing compared to the everlasting.
p: When we awake, when we are raised up, we will see Jesus, the express likeness of God. But, maybe David is also speaking of the glorified body we will receive in His likeness.

--- We shall be like him
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 17:15
scripture: 1 John 3:1-2 | mark: we shall be like him; for we shall see him as he is
p: This is our blessed hope. To awake into glory with Him. When we see Jesus as sons of God. Gathered up to the Lord, and with Him shall we ever be.

--- Peace in the next
kicker: Psalm 17 · Satisfied
p: Ultimately, this is the only satisfaction we need. If our enemies prosper, if God allows them to have advantage over us, while we suffer them, we know His grace is sufficient. That ultimately, vengeance will be His. That in the Lord's love for us, we will see Him as He is, and be like Him.
p: That no matter what may come in this life, we have peace in the next. Amen

